<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Viennese School</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Viennese School was a group of composers centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Arnold Schoenberg</a> and his pupils <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-berg">Alban Berg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-webern">Anton Webern</a> in early twentieth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. They developed atonality and later the twelve-tone method, transforming Western classical music. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> list the school among the cultural achievements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fin-de-siecle">fin-de-siècle</a> Vienna.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-viennese-school.jpg" alt="Second Viennese School" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Österreichische Musikzeitschrift · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wienerschule.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
